    "Polling dispersion, diaspora voting patterns, and entry of new parties fragment the vote, keeping the path to 133+ seats uncertain."
  ],
  "confidence": "low",
  "narrative": "Starting from a conservative baseline for a 2/3 majority, updates from opposition consolidation, system bias, and poll fragmentation shift the probability downward, with market signals and some polls offering marginal upside but insufficient to credibly approach a 2/3 outcome. The posterior remains small and uncertain, around 8%.",
  "justification": {
    "anchor": {
      "probability": 0.35,
      "basis": "reference class anchor: two-thirds majority in Hungary's National Assembly as the threshold; used to set a baseline near mid-range for 2/3 outcomes."
    },
    "factor_updates": [
      {
        "factor": "Opposition consolidation around TISZA",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Consolidation could raise anti-Orbán seat share, but gains depend on turnout and district dynamics; Bayesian updating nudges probability upward modestly, though still far from high confidence."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Electoral-system bias toward the largest bloc",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Mixed system and redistricting historically favor the largest bloc and complicate stacking a 133+ seat majority for any one coalition."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Polling dispersion and lack of consensus",
        "direction": "neutral",
        "magnitude": "weak",
        "rationale": "Divergent polls create uncertainty; the absence of a clear anchor reduces confidence in a 2/3 path."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Diaspora voting patterns and new entrants",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Diaspora tends to favor the governing bloc; additional entrants fragment votes, diluting potential majorities."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Market and macro signals pricing regime change",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "weak",
        "rationale": "Markets pricing in potential regime change suggest some upside to non-Orbán outcomes, but not decisively enough to push probability high."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis_rationale": "An anchor of 0.35 is adjusted downward by correlated factors (system bias, fragmentation) and modest upward adjustments from opposition consolidation and markets. Because factors are correlated (e.g., diaspora effects overlap with fragmentation), the net update remains small; the calibrated posterior at 0.08 reflects limited credible evidence for a 2/3 majority under current conditions and recognizes substantial uncertainty."
  }
}

